  "worked_solution": "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- The initial velocity of the apple is $u=0\\,\\text{m/s}.$\n\n- We can find the time it takes for the apple to fall in seconds as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =0.05\\,\\text{min} \\\\  & =0.05\\,\\text{min}\\cdot \\frac{60\\,\\text{s}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =0.05\\,\\cancel{\\text{min}}\\cdot \\frac{60\\,\\text{s}}{\\cancel{\\text{min}}} \\\\  & =3\\,\\text{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2} \\\\  & =0\\cdot 3+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 9.8\\cdot {3}^{2} \\\\  & =0+4.9\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =44.1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance covered by the apple is $44.1\\,\\text{m}.$",
